+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time to a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit: `d` for days, `h` for hours,
+`m` for minutes, and `s` for seconds. Each unit may appear at most once, in that order, with no spaces. Units are
+case-insensitive, so `1h30m`, `1H30M`, `90m`, and `5400` all mean the same duration. Negative numbers, decimals, and
+unknown, repeated, or out of order units are rejected.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+```
+
